<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Mediators Inflamm</journal-id><journal-title>Mediators of Inflammation</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0962-9351</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1466-1861</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">18472954</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2367052</article-id>		<article-id pub-id-type="pii">S0962935194000396</article-id>		<article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/S0962935194000396</article-id>	<article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Hyaluronic Acid Inhibits Polycation Induced Cellular Responses</article-title></title-group><contrib-group>		<contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>lalenti</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name></contrib>		<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>lanaro</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name></contrib>		<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Brignola</surname><given-names>G.</given-names></name></contrib>		<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Marotta</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name></contrib>		<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Di Rosa</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name></contrib>	</contrib-group>		<aff id="aff"><label/>			<addr-line>Department of Experimental Pharmacology</addr-line>			<addr-line>University of Naples &#x02018;Federico II&#x02019;</addr-line>			<addr-line>Via Domenico Montesano</addr-line>			<addr-line>Naples</addr-line>			<addr-line>49-80131</addr-line>			<country>Italy</country>		</aff>	<pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>1994</year></pub-date><volume>3</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>287</fpage><lpage>289</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1994 Hindawi Publishing Corporation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1994</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</copyright-holder><license license-type="open-access" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/"><p>This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</p></license></permissions><abstract>		<p>Positively charged macromolecules cause a variety of pathologicalevents through their electrostatic interaction with anionic sitespresent on the membrane of target cells. In the present study we haveinvestigated the effect of hyaluronic acid, a negatively chargedmolecule, on rat paw oedema induced by poly-L-lysine as well as onhistamine release from rat mast cells and nitric oxide formationfrom rabbit aorta, both induced by this polycation. The resultsindicate that hyaluronic acid is able to suppress thesepoly-L-lysine induced effects with a mechanism which possiblydepends on its negative charges which may balance the effects ofpositively charged polycations.</p>	</abstract></article-meta></front></article>